## Terminology ## {#terminology}

- <dfn>Dataset</dfn> refers to the definition given in [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] for `dcat:Dataset`.
- <dfn>Distribution</dfn> refers to the definition given in [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] for `dcat:Distribution`.
- <dfn>Data Service</dfn> refers to the definitions given in [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] for `dcat:DataService`.
- <dfn>Resource</dfn> refers to the generic term encompassing [=datasets=], [=distributions=] and [=data services=], defined in [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] as `dcat:Resource`
- <dfn>Catalog</dfn> refers to a [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] resource of type `dcat:Catalog`.
- <dfn>Shape</dfn> refers to a [[!SHACL]] `sh:NodeShape` that formally describes constraints over RDF data.
- <dfn>Discovery</dfn> refers to the process specified in [[#logical-flow]] for identifying relevant RDF [=resources=] over one or more [=catalogs=].
- <dfn>Client</dfn> refers to a client application that implements the [[#algorithm]] and supports the [[#logical-flow]].

# Describing DCAT(-AP) resource content with shapes # {#dcat-shapes}

This section defines a set of alternative ways in which DCAT(-AP) [=resources=], 
specifically referring to RDF [=datasets=] or [=data services=],
can be extended to include references to data [=shapes=] that semantically describe their content.  
The extensions are designed to be minimal and compatible with existing DCAT-AP profiles.

Note: The [[#logical-flow]] and related [[#algorithm]] are designed to support all the following data model extensions.
In the presence of more than one alternative, a [=discovery=] [=client=] will not prioritise any of them and should treat them as a logical OR relation.

Note: A [=shape=] linked to a [=resource=] may declare [[SHACL]] targets, such as `sh:targetClass`, to state which nodes
of the resource's data it describes. A [=discovery=] [=client=] takes them into account ([[#algorithm]]): a shape with
targets describes the nodes of its targets only, while a shape without targets is taken to describe any node.

## Relating to shapes via `dcterms:conformsTo` ## {#conforms-to}

In this approach we establish the use of the existing property `dcterms:conformsTo` [[!DCTERMS]], 
as described by [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] and [[!DCAT-AP]], to link an RDF [=resource=] defined as part of a [=catalog=],
to a [=shape=] that describes its content.

In [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]], the use of `dcterms:conformsTo` is informally suggested for linking a resource to a relevant standard,
that specifies its model, **schema**, ontology, application profile, among others.
SHACL shapes may be considered as a type of schema, as they define the structure and constraints that RDF instance data must adhere to.

No further clarifications are provided in [[!DCAT-AP]] regarding the expected use of `dcterms:conformsTo`,
beyond the informal suggestion to link to relevant standards, understood as instances of `dcterms:Standard`.

**Figure 1** shows how any kind of RDF [=resource=] that has been declared in a [=catalog=], may be linked to a related [=shape=] via `dcterms:conformsTo`.

<figure id="figure-1">
    <figcaption>
        DCAT Resources linked to a SHACL shape via `dcterms:conformsTo`. 
    </figcaption>
<xmp class=mermaid>
classDiagram
  direction TB
  class Dataset["dcat:Dataset"]:::dcat
  class Distribution["dcat:Distribution"]:::dcat
  class DataService["dcat:DataService"]:::dcat
  class Resource["dcat:Resource"]:::dcat
  class NodeShape["sh:NodeShape"]:::shacl

  Dataset --> Distribution : dcat#58;distribution
  Distribution --> DataService : dcat#58;accessService
  DataService --> Dataset : dcat#58;servesDataset
  Dataset --|> Resource
  DataService --|> Resource
  Distribution --> NodeShape : dcterms#58;conformsTo
  Resource --> NodeShape : dcterms#58;conformsTo

  classDef dcat fill:#ffe6cc,stroke:#d79b00
  classDef shacl fill:#dae8fc,stroke:#6c8ebf
</xmp>
</figure>

**Example 1** shows corresponding RDF representations for a [=dataset=], [=distribution=], and [=data service=], respectively linked to their [=shapes=].

<div class="example" id="example-1">
    <div class="tab-buttons">
        <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="showExample(1, 0)">Dataset Example</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showExample(1, 1)">Distribution Example</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showExample(1, 2)">Data Service Example</button>
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content">
    RDF representation in Turtle format of a `dcat:Dataset` linked to a `sh:NodeShape` via `dcterms:conformsTo`
    ```turtle
    ex:myDataset a dcat:Dataset;
        dcterms:title "My Dataset";
        dcterms:conformsTo ex:myShape.

    ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].
    ```
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" style="display:none;">
    RDF representation in Turtle format of a `dcat:Distribution` linked to a `sh:NodeShape` via `dcterms:conformsTo`
    ```turtle
    ex:myDataset a dcat:Dataset;
        dcterms:title "My Dataset";
        dcat:distribution ex:myDistribution.
        
    ex:myDistribution a dcat:Distribution;
        dcat:downloadURL <https://example.org/dataset.ttl>;
        dcat:mediaType <https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/text/turtle>;
        dcterms:conformsTo ex:myShape.

    ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].
    ```
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" style="display:none;">
    RDF representation in Turtle format of a `dcat:DataService` linked to a `sh:NodeShape` via `dcterms:conformsTo`
    ```turtle
    ex:myService a dcat:DataService;
        dcat:servesDataset ex:myDataset;
        dcat:endpointURL <https://example.org/my-data/service>;
        dcterms:title "My data service";
        dcterms:conformsTo ex:myShape, ex:myOtherShape.

    ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].

    ex:myOtherShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyOtherClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myOtherProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:integer;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].
    ```
    </div>
</div>

Note: A related [=shape=] to a [=data service=] could be referring to the constraints of either the input or output data (or both) of such service.
Via the `dcterms:conformsTo` property and under RDF v1.1, no distinction can be made about the role of a related shape
(see [[#qualified-relation]] for an alternative where it is possible). 
However, linking multiple shapes to a data service should not entail any conflicts for the [=discovery=] process, 
given its inclusive nature based on a logical OR operation. 

## Relating to shapes via `dcat:qualifiedRelation` ## {#qualified-relation}

In this approach we establish the use of the property `dcat:qualifiedRelation` as described by [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] and [[!DCAT-AP]], 
to indirectly link an RDF [=resource=] defined as part of a [=catalog=], to a [=shape=] that describes its content or the expected structure of input/output data for [=data services=].
This approach follows an n-ary [[SWBP-N-ARYRELATIONS]] or qualified [[LDPATTERNS]] data modelling pattern.

In [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]], the use of `dcat:qualifiedRelation` is informally specified as a way to define semantically richer relations
among resources. This is accomplished with the use of the `dcat:Relationship` class, on which specific roles can be set,
to further describe the nature of the relationship between a resource and another asset.
For specifying types of roles, the use of controlled vocabularies is encouraged, which is reflected by the existing subclass relation
between the `dcat:Role` class and `skos:Concept`.

The domain of `dcat:qualifiedRelation` is `dcat:Resource`, which includes DCAT [=datasets=] and [=data services=] but not [=distributions=].

No further guidelines nor usage notes are given for `dcat:qualifiedRelation` and `dcat:Relationship` in [[!DCAT-AP]].

**Figure 2** shows how a shape can be linked to any type of resource using the `dcat:qualifiedRelation` property.

<figure id="figure-2">
    <figcaption>
        DCAT Resources linked to a SHACL shape via `dcat:qualifiedRelation`. 
    </figcaption>
<xmp class=mermaid>
classDiagram
  direction TB
  class Dataset["dcat:Dataset"]:::dcat
  class DataService["dcat:DataService"]:::dcat
  class Resource["dcat:Resource"]:::dcat
  class Relationship["dcat:Relationship"]:::dcat
  class Role["dcat:Role"]:::dcat
  class Concept["skos:Concept"]:::skos
  class NodeShape["sh:NodeShape"]:::shacl

  DataService --> Dataset : dcat#58;servesDataset
  Dataset --|> Resource
  DataService --|> Resource
  Resource --> Relationship : dcat#58;qualifiedRelation
  Relationship --> NodeShape : dcterms#58;relation
  Relationship --> Role : dcat#58;hadRole
  Concept <|-- Role

  classDef dcat fill:#ffe6cc,stroke:#d79b00
  classDef skos fill:#d5e8d4,stroke:#82b366
  classDef shacl fill:#dae8fc,stroke:#6c8ebf
</xmp>
</figure>

In practice, for the [=discovery=] process, a [=client=] needs to traverse the property path `dcat:qualifiedRelation/dcterms:relation`,
in search for an associated [=shape=]. This specification does not mandate a specific role concept value via `dcat:hadRole`.
A discovery client must assess all existing qualified relations (see [[#logical-flow]]).

Note: [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] suggests the use of coded term lists such as [[IANA-RELATIONS]], or [[ISO-19115-1]],
to specify a `dcat:Relationship` role via the `dcat:hadRole` property.
However no established standard vocabulary currently exists for defining a role type that specifically conveys a relationship between a data resource and an associated [=shape=].
In section [[#roles]], we discuss how a discovery client may handle qualified relations to identify associated shapes.

**Example 2** shows corresponding RDF representations for a dataset and data service, respectively linked to their shapes via `dcat:qualifiedRelation`.
According to [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]], the domain of `dcat:qualifiedRelation` is `dcat:Resource`, which excludes `dcat:Distribution` instances.

<div class="example" id="example-2">
    <div class="tab-buttons">
        <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="showExample(2, 0)">Dataset Example</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showExample(2, 1)">Data Service Example</button>
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content">
    RDF representation in Turtle format of a `dcat:Dataset` linked to a `sh:NodeShape` via `dcat:qualifiedRelation/dcterms:relation`
    ```turtle
    ex:myDataset a dcat:Dataset;
        dcterms:title "My Dataset";
        dcat:qualifiedRelation [
            a dcat:Relationship;
            dcat:hadRole <http://www.iana.org/assignments/relation/describedby>; # this is optional
            dcterms:relation ex:myShape
        ].

    ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].
    ```
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" style="display:none;">
    RDF representation in Turtle format of a `dcat:DataService` linked to a `sh:NodeShape` via `dcat:qualifiedRelation/dcterms:relation`
    ```turtle
    ex:myService a dcat:DataService;
        dcat:endpointURL <https://example.org/my-data/service>;
        dcterms:title "My data service";
        dcat:qualifiedRelation [
            a dcat:Relationship;
            dcat:hadRole disc:InputShape; # this is optional
            dcterms:relation ex:myShape
        ], [
            a dcat:Relationship;
            dcat:hadRole disc:OutputShape; # this is optional
            dcterms:relation ex:myOtherShape
        ].

    ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].

    ex:myOtherShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:MyOtherClass;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:myOtherProperty;
            sh:datatype xsd:integer;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].
    ```
    </div>
</div>

Note: The qualified relation pattern allows for a more granular description of the type or relation
that a given shape has with a resource. For example, as it is shown in **Example 2 (Data Service Example)**,
where the qualified relations describe the shapes of both input and output data of a data service independently.
Roles may only influence the discovery process when defined as indicated in section [[#roles]].

## Relating to shapes via `sh:shapesGraph` ## {#shapes-graph}

In this approach we establish the use of the property `sh:shapesGraph`, as defined  by [[!SHACL]],
to link an RDF resource listed as part of a [=catalog=], to a shape that describes its content.

The `sh:shapesGraph` property is used in [[!SHACL]] to indicate a graph where the validation shapes are defined.
From the perspective of DCAT, the use of `sh:shapesGraph` constitutes a model extension 
that is not currently contemplated by [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]] nor [[!DCAT-AP]].

Note: An example of such an extension can be observed in the work of Franck Michel et al. [[WEB-API-DISC]],
where they propose the use of `sh:shapesGraph` to link a `dcat:Distribution` to a graph containing its shape definitions.

**Figure 3** shows how any kind of RDF resource that has been declared in a catalog, may be linked to a related shape via `sh:shapesGraph`.

<figure id="figure-3">
    <figcaption>
        DCAT Resources linked to a SHACL shape via `sh:shapesGraph`. 
    </figcaption>
<xmp class=mermaid>
classDiagram
  direction TB
  class Dataset["dcat:Dataset"]:::dcat
  class Distribution["dcat:Distribution"]:::dcat
  class DataService["dcat:DataService"]:::dcat
  class Resource["dcat:Resource"]:::dcat
  class ShapesGraph["ex:myShapesGraph"]:::namedgraph {
    <<shapes graph>>
  }
  class NodeShape["sh:NodeShape"]:::shacl

  Dataset --> Distribution : dcat#58;distribution
  Distribution --> DataService : dcat#58;accessService
  DataService --> Dataset : dcat#58;servesDataset
  Dataset --|> Resource
  DataService --|> Resource
  Distribution --> ShapesGraph : sh#58;shapesGraph
  Resource --> ShapesGraph : sh#58;shapesGraph
  ShapesGraph *-- NodeShape : contains

  classDef dcat fill:#ffe6cc,stroke:#d79b00
  classDef namedgraph fill:#b1ddf0,stroke:#10739e
  classDef shacl fill:#dae8fc,stroke:#6c8ebf
</xmp>
</figure>

An associated shapes graph linked via `sh:shapesGraph` may contain one or more shape definitions.
It is assumed that these must be related to the resource that links to them. 

**Example 3** shows corresponding RDF representations for a dataset, distribution, and data service, 
respectively linked to their shapes via `sh:shapesGraph`.

<div class="example" id="example-3">
    <div class="tab-buttons">
        <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="showExample(3, 0)">Dataset Example</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showExample(3, 1)">Distribution Example</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showExample(3, 2)">Data Service Example</button>
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content">
    RDF representation in TriG format of a `dcat:Dataset` linked to a shape via `sh:shapesGraph`
    ```turtle
    ex:myDataset a dcat:Dataset;
        dcterms:title "My Dataset";
        sh:shapesGraph ex:myShapesGraph.

    ex:myShapesGraph {
        ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
            sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
            sh:property [
                sh:path ex:myProperty;
                sh:datatype xsd:string;
                sh:minCount 1;
            ].
    }
    ```
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" style="display:none;">
    RDF representation in TriG format of a `dcat:Distribution` linked to a shape via `sh:shapesGraph`
    ```turtle
    ex:myDataset a dcat:Dataset;
        dcterms:title "My Dataset";
        dcat:distribution ex:myDistribution.
        
    ex:myDistribution a dcat:Distribution;
        dcat:downloadURL <https://example.org/dataset.ttl>;
        dcat:mediaType <https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/text/turtle>;
        sh:shapesGraph ex:myShapesGraph.

    ex:myShapesGraph {
        ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
            sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
            sh:property [
                sh:path ex:myProperty;
                sh:datatype xsd:string;
                sh:minCount 1;
            ].
    }
    ```
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" style="display:none;">
    RDF representation in TriG format of a `dcat:DataService` linked to a shape via `sh:shapesGraph`  
    ```turtle
    ex:myService a dcat:DataService;
        dcat:servesDataset ex:myDataset;
        dcat:endpointURL <https://example.org/my-data/service>;
        dcterms:title "My data service";
        sh:shapesGraph ex:myShapesGraph.

    ex:myShapesGraph {
        ex:myShape a sh:NodeShape;
            sh:targetClass ex:MyClass;
            sh:property [
                sh:path ex:myProperty;
                sh:datatype xsd:string;
                sh:minCount 1;
            ].

        ex:myOtherShape a sh:NodeShape;
            sh:targetClass ex:MyOtherClass;
            sh:property [
                sh:path ex:myOtherProperty;
                sh:datatype xsd:integer;
                sh:minCount 1;
            ].
    }
    ```
    </div>
</div>

# Shape relationship roles # {#roles}

Shape relationship roles provide guidance to a [=discovery=] [=client=] on wether assessing the relevance of a resource based on their [=shapes=]
is meaningful or not. They are applicable only to shape descriptions expressed through `dcat:qualifiedRelation`
([[#qualified-relation]]), where the role is the value of `dcat:hadRole` on the corresponding `dcat:Relationship`.

In [[!VOCAB-DCAT-3]], `dcat:Role` is a subclass of `skos:Concept` and the value of `dcat:hadRole` is a role *instance*. 
Accordingly, this specification defines the roles below as concepts of a <dfn export>shape role scheme</dfn>, identified by
`disc:ShapeRoles`:

- <dfn id="InputShape" export><code>disc:InputShape</code></dfn> identifies a [=shape=] that describes data accepted as input by a [=data service=].
- <dfn id="OutputShape" export><code>disc:OutputShape</code></dfn> identifies a [=shape=] that describes data produced as output by a [=data service=].

The following RDF statements formally express these definitions:

```turtle
@prefix dcat: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#>.
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>.
@prefix disc: <https://dishacled.github.io/discovery-specification/#>.
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>.

disc:ShapeRoles
	a skos:ConceptScheme ;
	dcterms:title "Shape relationship roles"@en ;
	skos:hasTopConcept disc:InputShape, disc:OutputShape .

disc:InputShape
	a dcat:Role, skos:Concept ;
	skos:prefLabel "Input shape role"@en ;
	skos:definition "A role identifying a data shape that describes data accepted as input by a data service."@en ;
	skos:topConceptOf disc:ShapeRoles ;
	skos:inScheme disc:ShapeRoles .

disc:OutputShape
	a dcat:Role, skos:Concept ;
	skos:prefLabel "Output shape role"@en ;
	skos:definition "A role identifying a data shape that describes data produced as output by a data service."@en ;
	skos:topConceptOf disc:ShapeRoles ;
	skos:inScheme disc:ShapeRoles .
```

The two concepts are disjoint: a single `dcat:Relationship` must not carry both `disc:InputShape` and `disc:OutputShape` as
values of `dcat:hadRole`. A [=client=] that encounters such contradiction must treat its role as undetermined and assess the
shape without role-based filtering.

When a user assigns one of these roles to the shape supplied in a discovery request (see [[#starting-from]]), a [=client=] should use
the complementary role to select the shapes of a candidate [=data service=] for relevance assessment:

- a user-provided shape with role `disc:InputShape` should be assessed against candidate shapes with role `disc:OutputShape` and must disregard shapes with role `disc:InputShape`.
- a user-provided shape with role `disc:OutputShape` should be assessed against candidate shapes with role `disc:InputShape` and must disregard shapes with role `disc:OutputShape`.

Discovery clients following this specification should also support application-specific roles declared as narrower concepts of
`disc:InputShape` and/or `disc:OutputShape` through `skos:broader`. Such a role must be treated according to the corresponding
broader role when selecting complementary candidate shapes. A role that is neither of these concepts nor transitively narrower
than one of them has no matching semantics defined by this specification and should not be used to exclude an otherwise
eligible shape.

<div class=example id="example-4">
An application-specific role that refines `disc:OutputShape`, and which a [=client=] must therefore treat as an output role.
```turtle
ex:GeoJSONOutputShape
	a dcat:Role, skos:Concept ;
	skos:prefLabel "GeoJSON output shape role"@en ;
	skos:broader disc:OutputShape .
```
</div>

If the user does not indicate a role for the provided shape, or a candidate shape has no `dcat:hadRole`, the [=client=]
must not exclude the candidate based on role. In that case, relevance is assessed without role-based filtering as specified
in [[#resource-relevance]].


# Discovery logical flow # {#logical-flow}

This section describes the logical steps that a client needs to follow to perform a discovery process.
An overview of the logical flow is shown in [Figure 4](#figure-4).

<figure id="figure-4">
    <figcaption>
        Overview of a discovery process's logical sequence. 
    </figcaption>
<xmp class=mermaid>
sequenceDiagram
  actor U as user
  participant I as :SHACL/SPARQL input
  participant A as discovery app
  participant C as DCAT(-AP) catalog
  participant R as RDF dataset / distribution / data service

  U->>A: 1️⃣ discovery request
  loop for each catalog
    A->>+C: 2️⃣ fetch catalog
    C-->>-A: return catalog quads
    loop for each resource
      A->>+R: 3️⃣ extract associated shape
      R-->>-A: return shape quads
      A->>+A: 4️⃣ assess resource
      I->>A: query/shape input
      deactivate A
    end
  end
  A->>U: 5️⃣ return matching resources
</xmp>
</figure>

In the next subsections a detailed description of each step is provided.

## Discovery request ## {#starting-from}

This is the initial step (labeled as 1️⃣ in [Figure 4](#figure-4)). A client starts a discovery process when it receives as input:
1. a set of formal constraints, expressed either as an <dfn>input SHACL shape</dfn> (in any RDF serialization ([string](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#javascript-string)))
    or an <dfn>input SPARQL query</dfn> ([string](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#javascript-string))
2. optionally, a <dfn>shape role</dfn> value for an [=input SHACL shape=], as defined in [[#roles]]
3. optionally, a set of one or more <dfn>catalog URL</dfn>s ([IRI](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3987#section-2)),
    which must point to a dereferenceable RDF representation of a [=catalog=]. Alternatively, a client may operate over previously retrieved catalogs.

Note: A [=shape role=] only makes sense when defined together with an [=input SHACL shape=].
It should be ignored if present together with an [=input SPARQL query=].

## Catalog retrieval ## {#catalog-retrieval}

This step (labeled as 2️⃣ in [Figure 4](#figure-4)) involves retrieving the RDF representation of each [=catalog=] provided as input.
A client must perform an HTTP GET request to each [=catalog URL=],
which must be dereferenceable to any RDF serialization (e.g. [[TURTLE]], [[JSON-LD11]], etc.).

Note: Even though in [Figure 4](#figure-4) the catalog retrieval step is shown as a sequential process,
a client may choose to perform the retrieval of multiple catalogs in parallel,
or to locally cache the retrieved catalogs,
to optimise the overall performance of the discovery process.

## Extraction of associated shapes ## {#extract-shapes}

This step (labeled as 3️⃣ in [Figure 4](#figure-4)) involves parsing the RDF representation of each retrieved [=catalog=],
to extract all the shapes associated with RDF resources declared in it.
A client must identify all resources of type `dcat:Dataset`, `dcat:Distribution`, and `dcat:DataService`,
and for each resource, extract any associated shape(s) using the three approaches defined in section [[#dcat-shapes]].
Given the mutually inclusive relation of these approaches, a client must consider all of them when extracting shapes.
That is, for every resource a client must:
1. follow the property path `dcterms:conformsTo` and assess the existence of `sh:NodeShape` instances ([[#conforms-to]]).
    The following SPARQL query expresses an equivalent operation to extract the relevant quads from a catalog instance.
    <div class='assertion'>
        <span class='marker'>EQUIVALENT SPARQL QUERY</span>
        ```sparql
        PREFIX sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#>
        PREFIX dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>
        PREFIX dcat: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#>

        SELECT ?resource ?shape WHERE {
            VALUES ?type { dcat:Dataset dcat:Distribution dcat:DataService }

            ?resource a ?type;
                dcterms:conformsTo ?shape.

            ?shape a sh:NodeShape.
        }
        ```
    </div>
2. follow the property path `dcat:qualifiedRelation/dcterms:relation` and assess the existence of `sh:NodeShape` instances ([[#qualified-relation]]).
    For each extracted shape, the client must also retain any provided [=shape role=] and assess it against the value of `dcat:hadRole` properties according to the rules defined in section [[#roles]].
    The following SPARQL query expresses an equivalent operation to extract the relevant quads from a catalog instance.
    <div class='assertion'>
        <span class='marker'>EQUIVALENT SPARQL QUERY</span>
        ```sparql
        PREFIX sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#>
        PREFIX dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>
        PREFIX dcat: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#>

        SELECT ?resource ?shape ?role WHERE {
            VALUES ?type { dcat:Dataset dcat:DataService }

            ?resource a ?type;
                dcat:qualifiedRelation ?relation.

            ?relation dcterms:relation ?shape.
            OPTIONAL { ?relation dcat:hadRole ?role. }

            ?shape a sh:NodeShape.
        }
        ```
    </div>
3. follow the property path `sh:shapesGraph` and assess the existence of `sh:NodeShape` instances within the referenced graph ([[#shapes-graph]]).
    The following SPARQL query expresses an equivalent operation to extract the relevant quads from a catalog instance.
    <div class='assertion'>
        <span class='marker'>EQUIVALENT SPARQL QUERY</span>
        ```sparql
        PREFIX sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#>
        PREFIX dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>
        PREFIX dcat: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#>
        
        SELECT ?resource ?shape WHERE {
            VALUES ?type { dcat:Dataset dcat:Distribution dcat:DataService }

            ?resource a ?type;
                sh:shapesGraph ?shapesGraph.

            GRAPH ?shapesGraph {
                ?shape a sh:NodeShape.
            }
        }
        ```
    </div>

Note: A catalog needs to be serialized in an RDF syntax that supports [=RDF datasets=] and [=named graphs=]
(e.g., N-Quads [[N-QUADS]], TriG [[TRIG]]) in order to support the [[#shapes-graph]] pattern.

The equivalent SPARQL queries listed above, make the assumption that all RDF quads for both resources and associated shapes,
are locally available as part of a catalog RDF document. However, it may be the case that a related shape is linked as a remote resource.
In such case, **a client must also dereference the shape's** [**IRI**](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3987#section-2) to retrieve its RDF representation.

## Relevance assessment ## {#resource-relevance}

This step (labeled as 4️⃣ in [Figure 4](#figure-4)) determines how relevant each resource is,
based on a containment assessment among their extracted shapes and the given 
[=input SHACL shape=] or [=input SPARQL query=] expressing the client's formal constraints.

A client must evaluate the containment relationship ([[#discovery-result]])
by applying the [=query-shape subsumption algorithm=] described in section [[#algorithm]].
The algorithm is applied once per candidate resource and yields one [=discovery result=] for it.

The [=resource shapes=] given to the algorithm are the shapes extracted for the candidate resource 
in step 3️⃣, that remain after role-based filtering if applicable ([[#roles]]). 
Role-based filtering does not apply to nested `sh:node` references. 
A role qualifies the `dcat:Relationship` between a resource and a shape, 
not the shape themselves (see [Example 5](#example-5)).

A client must resolve all nested shape references, including shapes removed by role-based filtering, 
since an unresolved reference can lower an otherwise [=contained=] result.

<div class=example id="example-5">
A geocoding [=data service=] takes postal addresses as input and returns the corresponding places, with their coordinates, as output.
Its output shape reuses its input shape through `sh:node` to describe the address of each place.
```turtle
ex:geocoder a dcat:DataService;
    dcterms:title "Geocoding service";
    dcat:endpointURL <https://example.org/geocode>;
    dcat:qualifiedRelation [
        a dcat:Relationship;
        dcat:hadRole disc:InputShape;
        dcterms:relation ex:AddressShape
    ], [
        a dcat:Relationship;
        dcat:hadRole disc:OutputShape;
        dcterms:relation ex:PlaceShape
    ].

ex:AddressShape a sh:NodeShape;
    sh:property [
        sh:path ex:streetAddress;
        sh:datatype xsd:string;
        sh:minCount 1;
    ], [
        sh:path ex:postalCode;
        sh:datatype xsd:string;
        sh:minCount 1;
    ].

ex:PlaceShape a sh:NodeShape;
    sh:targetClass ex:Place;
    sh:property [
        sh:path ex:latitude;
        sh:datatype xsd:decimal;
        sh:minCount 1;
    ], [
        sh:path ex:longitude;
        sh:datatype xsd:decimal;
        sh:minCount 1;
    ], [
        sh:path ex:address;
        sh:node ex:AddressShape;
        sh:minCount 1;
    ].
```
A discovery request provides the following [=input SHACL shape=], asking for places with a latitude and the postal code of their address,
together with the [=shape role=] `disc:InputShape`.
```turtle
ex:MyPlaceShape a sh:NodeShape;
    sh:targetClass ex:Place;
    sh:property [
        sh:path ex:latitude;
        sh:minCount 1;
    ], [
        sh:path ex:address;
        sh:node ex:MyAddressShape;
        sh:minCount 1;
    ].

ex:MyAddressShape a sh:NodeShape;
    sh:property [
        sh:path ex:postalCode;
        sh:minCount 1;
    ].
```
Given that role, the client disregards the `disc:InputShape` relationship of `ex:geocoder` ([[#roles]]),
so `ex:PlaceShape` is its only considered [=resource shape=].
The `sh:node` reference of `ex:PlaceShape` still resolves to `ex:AddressShape`, 
which subsumes the nested [=star pattern=] that leads to `ex:postalCode` in the [=input SHACL shape=],
and the service is classified as [=contained=].
If the client fully discarded `ex:AddressShape` along with its relationship, 
the [=star-pattern dependency=] on `ex:address` would remain unevaluated,
and the nested star pattern would only be compared with `ex:PlaceShape`, 
which is open and does not declare `ex:postalCode`.
That pattern, and therefore the service, would then be wrongly [=weakly rejected=].
</div>

## Presentation of results ## {#return-results}

In this last step, (labeled as 5️⃣ in [Figure 4](#figure-4)) the client returns an ordered (sub-)set of resource identifiers ([IRI](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3987#section-2))
from the considered data catalogs, labeled according to their relevance degree as specified in [[#discovery-result]], 
with respect to the given input constraints ([=input SHACL shape=] | [=input SPARQL query=]).

# Algorithm specification # {#algorithm}

This section defines a <dfn>query-shape subsumption algorithm</dfn> that aims to determine the <i>relevance</i> 
of an RDF [=resource=], described by a set of [=resource shapes=], 
towards a given set of formal constraints, expressed in the form of an [=input SHACL shape=] or an [=input SPARQL query=].
The algorithm decomposes the given input constraints into [=star patterns=] and compares each pattern with the
constraints expressed by the resource shapes. The result is expressed in the form of a qualified degree
that indicates whether the resource shapes contain, partially align with, or reject the input constraints ([[#discovery-result]]).

First, the inputs of the algorithm and the way it handles each kind of input are described,
followed by a set of preliminary concepts and the logical steps taken by the algorithm.

## Inputs ## {#algorithm-inputs}

The algorithm accepts the following inputs:

1. exactly one of:
    - an <dfn>input shape</dfn>: the user-provided SHACL shape defining the discovery constraints; or
    - an <dfn>input query</dfn>: the user-provided SPARQL query defining the discovery constraints; and
2. the <dfn>resource shapes</dfn>: the SHACL shapes extracted for a candidate RDF [=resource=] in [[#extract-shapes]] that
    remain after role-based filtering, as determined in [[#resource-relevance]]. Role-based filtering can leave none.

The algorithm evaluates both kinds of input in the same way. Its first step ([[#algorithm-steps]], step 1) brings either
of them into a common form, the <dfn>normalized input</dfn>: a set of [=triple patterns=], grouped into [=star patterns=]
that are linked by [=star-pattern dependencies=], together with the optional patterns, alternative branches, filters, and
[=negated triple patterns=] that qualify them.

- An [=input query=] is parsed, and its triple patterns are used as written.
- An [=input shape=] is translated. Each node shape, that is, the input shape and every shape it reaches through `sh:node`,
    becomes a star pattern about its focus node. Each of its property constraints becomes a triple pattern of that star
    pattern, which is optional when the constraint's `sh:minCount` is zero or absent, and each `sh:node` reference becomes
    a star-pattern dependency. Disjunctions become alternative branches, supported facets become filters, and
    [=negative property constraints=] become [=negated triple patterns=]. The targets of the input shape itself become
    patterns about its focus node, such as `rdf:type` for a class target; SHACL ignores the targets of the shapes it
    reaches through `sh:node`, and so does the translation.

From then on, the algorithm no longer distinguishes the two kinds of input. The definitions in [[#algorithm-preliminaries]]
are therefore stated over a query <math><mi>Q</mi></math>, which stands for the normalized input, whichever form the input
was given in. The [=resource shapes=], by contrast, are never translated: the algorithm compares the normalized input with
each of them as a [=normalized shape=]. The direction of that comparison is fixed: the input is the source and the resource
shapes are the candidates. An implementation must not reverse this orientation, since containment means that a
resource shape covers the constraints expressed by the input.

<div class=example>
An [=input shape=] asking for places with a latitude, an optional name, and an address with a postal code.
```turtle
ex:InputPlaceShape a sh:NodeShape;
    sh:property [
        sh:path ex:latitude;
        sh:datatype xsd:decimal;
        sh:minCount 1;
    ], [
        sh:path ex:name;
    ], [
        sh:path ex:address;
        sh:node ex:InputAddressShape;
        sh:minCount 1;
    ].

ex:InputAddressShape a sh:NodeShape;
    sh:property [
        sh:path ex:postalCode;
        sh:minCount 1;
    ].
```
Its [=normalized input=] is the same, up to variable names, as that of the following [=input query=], where `?place` and
`?address` stand for the focus nodes of `ex:InputPlaceShape` and `ex:InputAddressShape`.
```sparql
SELECT * WHERE {
    ?place ex:latitude ?latitude;
        ex:address ?address.
    OPTIONAL { ?place ex:name ?name. }
    ?address ex:postalCode ?postalCode.
    FILTER(datatype(?latitude) = xsd:decimal)
}
```
Both yield two [=star patterns=], about `?place` and `?address`, linked by a [=star-pattern dependency=] through
`ex:address`. The triple pattern on `ex:name` is optional, because its constraint has no `sh:minCount`, and the
`sh:datatype` facet becomes the filter on `?latitude`.
</div>

## Preliminaries ## {#algorithm-preliminaries}

Let <math><mi>I</mi></math>, <math><mi>B</mi></math>, <math><mi>L</mi></math>, and <math><mi>V</mi></math> denote the mutually disjoint
sets of IRIs, blank nodes, literals, and variables, respectively, and let a query <math><mi>Q</mi></math> denote the
[=normalized input=] ([[#algorithm-inputs]]), whether it was given as an [=input query=] or obtained from an [=input shape=].
The algorithm is specified using the following concepts, adapted from the formalization of query-shape subsumption in
[[TRAVELING-WITH-A-MAP]]:

- <dfn>triple pattern</dfn>: a tuple <math><mi>tp</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>tp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>tp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>tp</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> in <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>. 
    <math><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>tp</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>
    and <math><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>tp</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> denote its subject and object; <math><mi>O</mi></math> is undefined
    when the object is a literal, as it can't lead to further nested star patterns.
- <dfn>star pattern</dfn> [[STAR-PATTERNS]]: for a query <math><mi>Q</mi></math> and a subject <math><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>V</mi></math>, <math><msub><mi>SP</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>tp</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>tp</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>.
    In other words, a star pattern is the set of [=triple patterns=] of <math><mi>Q</mi></math> sharing subject <math><mi>x</mi></math>.
    <div class=example>
    Example of a [=star pattern=] defined as part of a SPARQL query.
    ```sparql
    SELECT * WHERE {
        ?subject a ex:Entity;  #--|
            ex:pred1 ?obj1;    #  | star pattern
            ex:pred2 ?obj2.    #--|
    }
    ```
    </div>
- <dfn export>graph star pattern</dfn>: for a query <math><mi>Q</mi></math> and a root <math><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>V</mi></math>
    with <math><msub><mi>SP</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>∅</mi></math>,
    the graph star pattern <math><msub><mi>GSP</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> is the least set <math><mi>G</mi></math> such that
    (i) <math><msub><mi>SP</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></math>, and 
    (ii) <math><msub><mi>SP</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>tp</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></math>
    for every triple pattern <math><mi>tp</mi></math> in a star pattern of <math><mi>G</mi></math> such that <math><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>tp</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> is defined
    and <math><msub><mi>SP</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>tp</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>∅</mi></math>.
    
    For each such <math><mi>tp</mi></math>, the pair <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>tp</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>SP</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>tp</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>
    is a <dfn>star-pattern dependency</dfn>.
    
    In other words, a graph star pattern contains a designated root [=star pattern=] and, recursively, every star pattern
    whose subject is the non-literal object of a triple pattern in a star pattern already in the set. 
    This transitive definition includes dependencies at arbitrary depth, not only direct children of the root.
    <div class=example>
    Example of a [=graph star pattern=] defined as part of a SPARQL query.
    ```sparql
    SELECT * WHERE {
        ?subject a ex:Entity;  #--|-------------------|
            ex:pred1 ?obj1;    #  | root star pattern |
            ex:pred2 ?obj2.    #--|                   |
                               #                      |
        ?obj1 a ex:SubEntity;  #--|                   |
            ex:pred3 ?obj3;    #  | star pattern      | graph star pattern
            ex:pred4 ?obj4.    #--|                   |
                               #                      |
        ?obj3 a ex:SubEntity2; #--|                   |
            ex:pred5 ?obj5;    #  | star pattern      |
            ex:pred6 ?obj6.    #--|-------------------|
    }
    ```
    </div>
- <dfn>normalized shape</dfn>: a tuple <math><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Ign</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>
    that represents a SHACL node shape, where:

    - its identifier is <math><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi></math>;
    - its status is <math><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>open</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>closed</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>: closed exactly
        when its `sh:closed` value is `true`, and open otherwise;
    - its positive [=property constraints=] form a finite set <math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></math>, with one property constraint for each
        value of `sh:property` that does not yield a [=negative property constraint=];
    - the predicates of its [=negative property constraints=] form the set <math><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>I</mi></math>;
    - its disjunctions form a finite set <math><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></math>, with one disjunction for each value of `sh:or` or `sh:xone`.
        A disjunction is a set of branches, one for each member of the list, and a branch is the set of property
        constraints of that member. `sh:xone` is read as `sh:or`, which admits every node that `sh:xone` admits;
    - its [=shape targets=] form the set <math><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></math>; and
    - its ignored properties form the set <math><msub><mi>Ign</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>I</mi></math> of the properties listed by
        `sh:ignoredProperties` when the shape is closed, and the empty set otherwise.

    A <dfn>property constraint</dfn> is a tuple <math><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>
    obtained from a SHACL property shape whose `sh:path` is an IRI. Its predicate is that IRI, <math><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></math>;
    its [=object constraints=] form a finite set <math><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></math>; and its minimum and maximum counts are the values
    of `sh:minCount` and `sh:maxCount`, <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></math> and <math><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>,
    or 0 and ∞ when they are absent, as in [[SHACL]]. A property constraint is required when <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.
    Its maximum count is at least 1, because a property shape whose `sh:maxCount` is 0 yields a
    [=negative property constraint=] instead.

    An <dfn>object constraint</dfn> restricts the values of a property, and is one of:

    - a datatype constraint <math><mi>datatype</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>, for a value <math><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></math>
        of `sh:datatype`;
    - a class constraint <math><mi>class</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>, for a value <math><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></math>
        of `sh:class`;
    - a shape reference <math><mi>node</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>, for a value <math><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi></math>
        of `sh:node`, which refers to the normalized shape whose identifier is <math><mi>n</mi></math>;
    - a numeric bound <math><mi>bound</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>, for a numeric value <math><mi>a</mi></math>
        of `sh:minInclusive`, `sh:minExclusive`, `sh:maxInclusive`, or `sh:maxExclusive`, where <math><mi>θ</mi></math> is
        ≥, &gt;, ≤, or &lt;, respectively;
    - a regular expression <math><mi>pattern</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>, for a value <math><mi>r</mi></math>
        of `sh:pattern` and the value <math><mi>f</mi></math> of `sh:flags`, empty when it is absent; or
    - a value set <math><mi>in</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>, for a set <math><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>I</mi></math>, which only the
        members of <math><mi>K</mi></math> satisfy, and which only [=implied property constraints=] carry.

    The property constraints of the branches of a shape are <math><mi>br</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo>⋃</mo><mi>d</mi></math>, and the
    normalized shapes that it references are <math><mi>refs</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>node</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>br</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>.

    A <dfn>negative property constraint</dfn> states that conforming data must **not** carry a property, and is therefore
    the only constraint of an open shape that can positively establish incompatibility. It is either a property shape whose
    `sh:maxCount` is 0, or a value of `sh:not` that is a property shape whose only parameters are an IRI `sh:path` and
    `sh:minCount 1`: a node conforms to that negation exactly when it has no value for the path. A value of `sh:not` in any
    other form does not forbid its property, and is ignored. For example, `sh:not [ sh:path ex:age; sh:minCount 2 ]`
    admits a node with one value of `ex:age`, and `sh:not [ sh:path ex:age; sh:datatype xsd:string ]` requires a value of
    `ex:age` that is not a string. Ignoring a negation only widens the data that a shape admits, so it never causes a
    rejection.

    The <dfn>shape targets</dfn> of a shape are its [[SHACL]] targets, which <math><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></math> represents as pairs:

    - a node target <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>targetNode</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> for each value <math><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>L</mi></math> of `sh:targetNode`;
    - a class target <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>targetClass</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> for each value <math><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></math> of `sh:targetClass`, and for <math><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></math> when the shape is
        also an `rdfs:Class`;
    - a subjects-of target <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>targetSubjectsOf</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> for each value <math><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></math> of `sh:targetSubjectsOf`; and
    - an objects-of target <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>targetObjectsOf</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> for each value <math><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></math> of `sh:targetObjectsOf`.

    In an RDF graph <math><mi>G</mi></math>, the focus nodes of a shape are the union, over every target <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></math>, of:

    - the node <math><mi>v</mi></math> itself, for a node target;
    - the set <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>rdf:type</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>
        of [[SHACL]] instances of <math><mi>v</mi></math>, for a class target, where <math><mi>k</mi></math> ranges over <math><mi>v</mi></math> and its subclasses in <math><mi>G</mi></math>;
    - the set <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>,
        for a subjects-of target; and
    - the set <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>,
        for an objects-of target.

    Shape targets state which nodes a shape describes when it is used on its own; SHACL ignores them when the shape is
    reached through `sh:node`, and so does the algorithm. A shape used on its own has the <dfn>implied property constraints</dfn> of its shape targets. With <math><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>targetClass</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>
    its target classes, they form the set <math><mi>imp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> that contains:

    - the constraint <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>rdf:type</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>in</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>
        when <math><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>∅</mi></math>, where <math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> when all the shape targets are
        class targets, and <math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> otherwise; and
    - a constraint <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>∅</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> for each <math><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>targetSubjectsOf</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></math>, where <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> when <math><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>targetSubjectsOf</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>,
        and <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> otherwise.

    An implied property constraint is thus required only when every focus node carries it. Class targets are compared by
    IRI only, through <math><mi>in</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> rather than a class constraint, because the class
    hierarchy is described in data that a client does not see.

    The <dfn>shape body</dfn> of a shape used on its own is <math><mi>body</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>imp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>∅</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Ign</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>,
    and that of a shape reached through `sh:node` is the same set without <math><mi>imp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>: ignored properties count as
    optional property constraints that leave their values unrestricted. The <dfn>declared predicates</dfn> of a shape are <math><mi>decl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>body</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi>br</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>, and a closed shape
    allows exactly these. They include every property that [[SHACL]] allows on a closed shape and that the shape does not
    forbid, and may include more, because SHACL counts neither the branches of a disjunction nor implied property
    constraints; a closed shape is therefore never taken to forbid a property that SHACL allows. Together, <math><mi>body</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></math> play the roles of the basic graph pattern and the union graph patterns of the
    query <math><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> that [[TRAVELING-WITH-A-MAP]] obtains from the shape.
    <div class=example>
    A SHACL node shape describing persons, followed by the components of its [=normalized shape=] <math><mi>S</mi></math>.
    ```turtle
    ex:PersonShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetClass ex:Person;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:name;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ], [
            sh:path ex:age;
            sh:datatype xsd:integer;
            sh:minInclusive 0;
        ], [
            sh:path ex:address;
            sh:node ex:AddressShape;
        ];
        sh:or (
            [ sh:path ex:email; sh:minCount 1 ]
            [ sh:path ex:phone; sh:minCount 1 ]
        );
        sh:not [
            sh:path ex:password;
            sh:minCount 1;
        ].
    ```
    - identifier: <math><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ex:PersonShape</mi></math>;
    - status: <math><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>open</mi></math>, since the shape does not declare `sh:closed true`;
    - positive property constraints: <math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>, where

        - on `ex:name`, <math><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ex:name</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>datatype</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>xsd:string</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>;
        - on `ex:age`, <math><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ex:age</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>datatype</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>xsd:integer</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>bound</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≥</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>; and
        - on `ex:address`, <math><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ex:address</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>node</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ex:AddressShape</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>;

    - negative property constraints: <math><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ex:password</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>;
    - disjunctions: <math><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>, with the branches <math><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ex:email</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>∅</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math> and <math><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ex:phone</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>∅</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>;
    - references to other normalized shapes: <math><mi>refs</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ex:AddressShape</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>;
    - shape targets: <math><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>targetClass</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ex:Person</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>, which imply <math><mi>imp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>rdf:type</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>in</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ex:Person</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></math>,
        required because the only shape target is a class target; and
    - ignored properties: <math><msub><mi>Ign</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>∅</mi></math>, since the shape is open.

    Used on its own, the shape has the [=shape body=] <math><mi>body</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>imp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>.

    </div>
- <dfn>pattern-shape match</dfn>: a relation <math><mi>tp</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>c</mi></math> between a [=triple pattern=] <math><mi>tp</mi></math> and a [=property constraint=] <math><mi>c</mi></math> of a [=normalized shape=]. Their predicates must be equal, <math><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>tp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></math>.
    Explicit object restrictions of the input must satisfy the [=object constraints=] <math><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></math>, and the filters retained for it
    must not be provably contradictory. An object variable for which no applicable restriction is available is compatible rather than
    contradictory. A [=negative property constraint=] never yields a [=pattern-shape match=]: a triple pattern whose predicate
    the shape forbids is instead a <dfn>negative contradiction</dfn> for that shape. Conversely, a [=negated triple pattern=]
    whose predicate the shape requires is a negative contradiction as well.
    This is a conservative approximation of the paper's `match` relation, which requires the domain of triples
    matched by the query pattern to be a subset of the domain permitted by the shape pattern.
    <div class=example>
    Triple patterns compared with the property constraints of `ex:PersonShape` from the previous example.
    Each line is a separate triple pattern, together with the filter retained for it, if any.
    ```sparql
    ?p ex:age ?age.                    # match: ?age is unrestricted
    ?p ex:age 42.                      # match: an xsd:integer of at least 0
    ?p ex:age ?age. FILTER(?age > 30)  # match: compatible with sh:minInclusive 0
    ?p ex:email ?email.                # match: in the first sh:or branch
    ?p a ex:Person.                    # match: implied by the class target
    ?p ex:age "unknown".               # no match: not an xsd:integer
    ?p ex:age ?age. FILTER(?age < 0)   # no match: contradicts sh:minInclusive 0
    ?p ex:nickname ?nickname.          # no match: ex:nickname is not constrained
    ?p a ex:Place.                     # no match: not the target class
    ?p ex:password ?password.          # no match: negative contradiction (sh:not)
    ```
    </div>
- <dfn>negated triple pattern</dfn>: a [=triple pattern=] that the input requires to have **no** match. It is obtained
    from a `FILTER NOT EXISTS`, or `FILTER(!EXISTS …)`, or a `MINUS`, whose graph pattern is a single triple pattern such
    that: its predicate is an IRI; its subject is a variable that is the subject of a required triple pattern of the group
    the negation applies to, which is the filtered group or the left operand of `MINUS`; and its object is a blank node or
    a variable that does not occur in that group. Under these conditions `FILTER NOT EXISTS` and `MINUS` remove the same
    solutions: those whose subject carries the predicate, whatever its value. A [=negative property constraint=] of an
    [=input shape=] is translated to a negated triple pattern too.
    A negated triple pattern belongs to the [=star pattern=] of its subject, but it is never required and never yields a
    [=pattern-shape match=]. A shape <math><mi>S</mi></math> whose [=shape body=] requires its predicate <math><mi>p</mi></math>, that is, has a
    constraint <math><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>body</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> with <math><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></math> and <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, is a [=negative contradiction=] for it,
    because every node that conforms to the shape carries the predicate. A shape that forbids the predicate, with <math><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></math>,
    or is closed and lacks it among its [=declared predicates=], with <math><mi>p</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>decl</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>, satisfies the negation, and any other
    shape leaves it undecided.
    <div class=example>
    Negations compared with `ex:PersonShape` from the previous examples. Each line follows the triple pattern
    `?p ex:name ?name`, which binds `?p`.
    ```sparql
    FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?p ex:password ?pw }  # negated: satisfied by sh:not
    FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?p ex:age ?age }      # negated: undecided
    MINUS { ?p ex:name [] }                   # negated: negative contradiction
    MINUS { ?x ex:password ?pw }              # discarded: shares no variable
    FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?p ex:name ?name }    # discarded: ?name is bound
    ```
    The third line contradicts `ex:PersonShape`, which requires `ex:name` through `sh:minCount 1`. The last two lines
    are not negated triple patterns, and are discarded as described in [[#out-of-profile]].
    </div>
- <dfn export>star-shape subsumption</dfn>: a [=star pattern=] is subsumed by a normalized shape when every **required** triple
    pattern has a [=pattern-shape match=] in the [=shape body=] or in one accepted disjunctive branch, at least one triple pattern
    has such a match, and every [=star-pattern dependency=]
    is recursively subsumed by the referenced shape. Implementations must cache visited star/shape pairs, or use an equivalent
    mechanism, so cyclic dependencies terminate. A [=negated triple pattern=] needs no match, but a shape for which it is a
    [=negative contradiction=] does not subsume the star pattern. A shape used on its own whose [=shape targets=] are all
    node targets describes those nodes only, so it does not subsume a star pattern whose subject is a constant that is
    none of them.
    A triple pattern is required unless it originates from an `OPTIONAL` graph pattern of an [=input query=], or from a
    property constraint of an [=input shape=] whose `sh:minCount` is zero or, as in [[SHACL]], absent. Optional triple
    patterns never prevent subsumption, because a resource can satisfy the input without them. This rule is the same for
    open and closed shapes.
    <div class=example>
    The star pattern of `?person` in the following query is subsumed by `ex:PersonShape` from the previous examples,
    given the shape `ex:AddressShape` below.
    ```sparql
    SELECT * WHERE {
        ?person ex:name ?name;
            ex:email ?email;
            ex:address ?address.
        OPTIONAL { ?person ex:nickname ?nickname. }
        ?address ex:city ?city.
    }
    ```
    ```turtle
    ex:AddressShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:city;
            sh:datatype xsd:string;
        ].
    ```
    The required triple patterns on `ex:name` and `ex:address` match constraints of the shape body, and the one on
    `ex:email` matches the first branch of its disjunction. The triple pattern on `ex:nickname` matches nothing, but it is
    optional. The [=star-pattern dependency=] through `ex:address` is subsumed by `ex:AddressShape`, which matches `ex:city`.
    The subsumption would no longer hold if the query also required `?person ex:birthDate ?birthDate`, which
    `ex:PersonShape` does not declare, or `?address ex:zip ?zip`, which `ex:AddressShape` does not declare.
    </div>
- <dfn>describes</dfn>: a [=resource shape=] describes a [=star pattern=] when its closedness and its
    [=negative property constraints=] apply to every node that matches the star pattern. A shape without
    [=shape targets=] describes every star pattern, and a shape reached through `sh:node` describes the nested star pattern
    it resolves. A shape used on its own that has shape targets constrains their nodes only, so it describes a star
    pattern only when every node matching the star pattern is one of its focus nodes. This is the case when the star
    pattern (i) has as subject a constant, or one of several constants, that are all node targets of the shape;
    (ii) requires `rdf:type` with values that are all target classes of the shape; (iii) requires the predicate of a
    subjects-of target of the shape; or (iv) is reached through a required triple pattern whose predicate is that of an
    objects-of target of the shape. A shape that does not describe a star pattern says nothing about the nodes it asks
    for: it neither supports nor prevents the rejection of the star pattern, and its matches count as on an open shape
    ([[#algorithm-steps]], step 4).
    <div class=example>
    A closed shape that describes `ex:Alice` only.
    ```turtle
    ex:AliceShape a sh:NodeShape;
        sh:targetNode ex:Alice;
        sh:closed true;
        sh:property [
            sh:path ex:name;
        ].
    ```
    Star patterns compared with `ex:AliceShape`, each on its own.
    ```sparql
    ex:Alice ex:latitude ?latitude.  # described: rejected
    ex:Bob ex:latitude ?latitude.    # not described: weakly rejected
    ?x ex:latitude ?latitude.        # not described: weakly rejected
    ```
    The shape forbids `ex:latitude` for `ex:Alice`, which it describes, so the first star pattern is [=rejected=]. It
    says nothing about `ex:Bob`, nor about the nodes that `?x` may stand for, so the other two are [=weakly rejected=].
    </div>

The Boolean relation <math><mi>Q</mi><msub><mo>⊑</mo><mi>qs</mi></msub><mi>S</mi></math> from
[[TRAVELING-WITH-A-MAP]] establishes query relevance when at least one [=star pattern=] of at least one [=graph star pattern=]
of <math><mi>Q</mi></math>, taken as a root, is subsumed by the query obtained from shape <math><mi>S</mi></math>. This specification retains that local relation as the basis of a full
match, but extends its Boolean output with partial-evidence classifications. In particular, [=contained=] below is deliberately
stronger: it requires every normalized star pattern of the discovery input to be subsumed by at least one resource shape.

This specification departs from [[TRAVELING-WITH-A-MAP]] in two respects:

**Open shapes are graded instead of being treated as unconstrained.** [[TRAVELING-WITH-A-MAP]] translates an open shape into a query
over the entire knowledge graph, on the grounds that such a shape imposes only the minimal constraints its data must satisfy;
subsumption by an open shape is consequently always satisfied. That treatment is appropriate for link pruning, where the only
question is whether a resource may be skipped. Discovery instead has to rank resources that cannot be excluded, so this
specification distinguishes degrees of partial evidence over open shapes: [=aligned=] when some of the input is positively
covered, and [=weakly rejected=] when none of it is but the shape does not exclude it either. The complete scale is defined in
[[#discovery-result]]. Only [=rejected=] carries the exclusion force of the Boolean relation.

**Resource shapes are not translated into queries.** [[TRAVELING-WITH-A-MAP]] compares two queries, having obtained
<math><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> from the shape. This specification compares a
query against shape constraints directly, as a [=normalized shape=] ([[#algorithm-steps]], step 2). The translation to a query
is applied only to an [=input shape=], to obtain its [=normalized input=] ([[#algorithm-inputs]]). The comparison is otherwise
the same relation, and the direction is fixed: the input is the source and the resource shapes are the candidates.

## Logical steps ## {#algorithm-steps}

Given the inputs described in [[#algorithm-inputs]], the algorithm proceeds as follows:

1. **Normalize the input constraints** into the [=normalized input=].
    1. An [=input query=] is parsed and its [=triple patterns=] are grouped by subject into [=star patterns=].
        When the object of a triple pattern is also the subject of another star pattern, the latter is recorded as a dependency
        of the former. Supported `OPTIONAL`, `UNION`, `VALUES`, property-path, and `FILTER` forms, and
        [=negated triple patterns=], are retained as described in [[#algorithm-profile]]; constructs outside that profile are
        discarded, as specified in [[#out-of-profile]].
    2. An [=input shape=] is translated to the same representation. Each node shape, that is, the input shape and every shape
        it reaches through `sh:node`, becomes a star pattern whose subject is a variable standing for its focus node.
        Positive SHACL property constraints become triple patterns of that star pattern, optional when their `sh:minCount` is
        zero or absent; `sh:node` relations become star-pattern dependencies; `sh:or` and `sh:xone` become alternative
        branches; supported datatype, numeric-bound, and regular-expression facets become filter constraints; and
        [=negative property constraints=] become [=negated triple patterns=]. `sh:closed` is not translated.
        The [=shape targets=] of the input shape itself, but not those of the shapes it reaches through `sh:node`, become
        patterns about its focus node: a class target requires `rdf:type` with the class as value; a subjects-of target
        requires its predicate; node targets restrict the subject to the target nodes; and an objects-of target adds a
        star pattern with a triple pattern on its predicate whose object is the focus node, so that the focus star pattern
        is a dependency of it. Several class and subjects-of targets become alternative branches, as for `sh:or`, because
        the focus nodes are the nodes of any of the targets. Node and objects-of targets combined with other targets, and
        several objects-of targets, cannot be translated and are discarded ([[#out-of-profile]]). A star pattern left
        without any triple pattern constrains nothing and is removed; when its subject was restricted to node targets,
        the input asks for any triple about those nodes, as an [=unrestricted triple pattern=] does (step 1.3).
    3. A triple pattern whose predicate is a variable is an <dfn>unrestricted triple pattern</dfn>. It yields no
        [=star pattern=], because a [=shape=] constrains named predicates, but it asks for every triple of a resource.
        An input whose triple patterns are all unrestricted is therefore [=contained=] by any [=resource shape=], and every
        resource shape must be marked visitable: the input is maximally unselective, which is a property of the input and not
        a failure to assess it.
    4. Normalization must otherwise yield at least one [=star pattern=]. An input that yields none and contains no
        [=unrestricted triple pattern=] never asks the resource for data — its answer is the same whichever resource is
        considered — so relevance is undefined and a client must not assign it a [=discovery result=]. An empty `WHERE`
        clause, a body of only `VALUES` or `FILTER`, and a body left empty after discarding out-of-profile constructs
        ([[#out-of-profile]]) are of this kind.
2. **Normalize the resource shapes.** Each [=resource shape=], and each shape it reaches through `sh:node`, becomes a
    [=normalized shape=]. A resource shape compared with a star pattern directly, rather than reached through `sh:node`, is
    used on its own, so its [=shape body=] includes the [=implied property constraints=] of its [=shape targets=].
3. **Bind star patterns to resource shapes.** For every normalized star pattern and every resource shape, the algorithm:
    1. computes [=pattern-shape matches=] for predicates and supported object constraints;
    2. checks compatible datatype, class, numeric-bound, and regular-expression constraints;
    3. recursively evaluates dependent star patterns against referenced shapes, while tracking visited dependencies to terminate cycles.
          A [=star-pattern dependency=] that is not established is <dfn>refuted</dfn> only when the referenced shape leaves a
          required nested triple pattern unmatched while being closed, or yields a [=negative contradiction=]. When the
          referenced shape is open and merely does not declare what the nested pattern asks for, the dependency is undecided rather than
          refuted: the predicate of the referring triple pattern still matched the resource shape, which step 4 counts as a
          partial match. A shape reached from a resource shape through `sh:node`, directly or transitively, also governs the
          nested star pattern it resolves: step 4 must classify that pattern against it as against a [=resource shape=],
          whether or not it is one;
    4. evaluates `UNION` or SHACL disjunction branches; and
    5. checks whether retained filters contradict the resource shape constraints. A filter that cannot be decided from shape
          information alone must not cause rejection; a provable contradiction prevents that shape from contributing a match; and
    6. checks for a [=negative contradiction=]. A resource shape that forbids the predicate of a required triple pattern, or
          requires the predicate of a [=negated triple pattern=], must not contribute a match, and must not be counted as an
          open shape in step 4, because it establishes incompatibility rather than remaining silent about the property.
4. **Classify each star pattern.** Assign the first applicable result in the following order. A resource shape that does
    not [=describes|describe=] the star pattern counts as open for it.
    1. [=contained=], if the root star pattern and all its required nested patterns are subsumed. A single resource shape is
        normally responsible for this, but a disjunctive branch left unresolved by that shape may be subsumed by another
        resource shape of the same candidate resource;
    2. [=aligned=], if at least one triple in the root star pattern binds to an open resource shape;
    3. [=unaligned=], if a root triple binds only to a closed resource shape, or if only a nested star pattern binds. A root
        triple whose predicate matched a closed resource shape but whose [=star-pattern dependency=] was undecided rather than
        [=refuted=] counts as binding for this purpose;
    4. [=weakly rejected=], if no triple binds and at least one resource shape that [=describes=] the star pattern is
        open, or no resource shape describes it; or
    5. [=rejected=], if no triple binds and every resource shape that [=describes=] the star pattern, of which there is at
        least one, is closed or yields a [=negative contradiction=], or there is no resource shape at all. A star pattern
        must not be [=rejected=] on the strength of shapes that do not describe it.
5. **Aggregate the result.** The result for the complete input is the least favorable result assigned to any of its star patterns,
    according to the ordering defined in [[#discovery-result]]. Consequently, the complete input is [=contained=] only when every
    star pattern is [=contained=].

The algorithm returns a [=discovery result=] containing the aggregate result, the result and matching shape IRIs for each
star pattern, the corresponding predicate-level bindings, and a visit indication for every resource shape. A resource shape
is marked as visitable when at least one input triple pattern binds to it. This indication is independent from the aggregate
result and may be used by a client to decide whether to retrieve data associated with the shape.

## Supported constraint profile ## {#algorithm-profile}

Conforming implementations must document the SPARQL and SHACL fragment they evaluate. The reference implementation supports:

- SPARQL basic triple patterns with IRI predicates; `OPTIONAL`; `UNION`; object-variable substitution from `VALUES`;
    alternative paths; negated property sets; and the `?`, `*`, and `+` path cardinality forms over a single IRI path.
    Sequence and inverse paths are partially normalized when they occur as branches of an alternative path;
- `FILTER NOT EXISTS`, `FILTER(!EXISTS …)`, and `MINUS` over a single triple pattern, as [=negated triple patterns=];
- conservative `FILTER` compatibility for equality and inequality comparisons, numeric bounds, conjunction, disjunction,
    and regular expressions; an undecidable expression produces unknown evidence and must not produce rejection; and
- SHACL property paths represented by IRIs, `sh:closed`, `sh:node`, `sh:class`, `sh:datatype`, `sh:or`, `sh:xone`,
    numeric bounds, `sh:pattern` with optional flags, and both `sh:maxCount 0` and `sh:not` over a property shape whose
    only parameters are `sh:path` and `sh:minCount 1`, each of which yields a [=negative property constraint=]; `sh:ignoredProperties` of a closed shape; and the [[SHACL]] targets
    `sh:targetNode`, `sh:targetClass`, implicit class targets, `sh:targetSubjectsOf`, and `sh:targetObjectsOf`
    ([=shape targets=]), with class targets compared by IRI only.

The reference implementation records path and SHACL cardinalities, reading an absent `sh:minCount` as zero and an absent
`sh:maxCount` as unbounded, as [[SHACL]] does. It treats a minimum cardinality of zero as optional, and a minimum of at
least one in the shape body as requiring the property for [=negated triple patterns=], but does not otherwise compare
cardinality ranges during binding. `sh:not` in any other form, which is ignored,
top-level sequence or inverse paths, and arbitrarily nested property paths are outside the supported profile.

Note: This specification does not define the use of ShEx [[SHEX-PRIMER]] to describe resource content. The reference implementation
additionally accepts ShEx shapes and normalizes them to the same [=normalized shape=] representation, but only the SHACL
fragment listed above is in scope here.

### Handling constructs outside the profile ### {#out-of-profile}

A construct outside the supported profile must be **discarded** during normalization: it must not contribute [=triple patterns=]
to the [=normalized input=]. In particular, a client must discard `MINUS` and `NOT EXISTS` graph patterns that do not form
a [=negated triple pattern=], `SERVICE` clauses, sub-`SELECT`s and aggregates, and `BIND`. A discarded `MINUS` keeps its
left operand. Any other `EXISTS` or `NOT EXISTS` expression is discarded by treating it as undecidable: the rest of the
`FILTER` expression that contains it is still evaluated, but the existence test itself must not cause rejection.

Note: Outside the form of a [=negated triple pattern=], `MINUS` and `NOT EXISTS` no longer remove the same solutions, and
shape constraints cannot decide either of them. A `MINUS` that shares no variable with its left operand removes nothing,
whereas a `NOT EXISTS` whose subject is unbound tests the whole resource. A constant object, or one bound by the group,
restricts the negation to particular values, which the supported profile cannot compare.

Note: An [=unrestricted triple pattern=] is not a discarded construct. It contributes no [=star pattern=], but it does express
a constraint — that the resource holds triples — which every resource satisfies. See [[#algorithm-steps]].

Discarding a construct removes constraints from the input, so the resulting [=discovery result=] is computed over a sub-input
of the one given. This can only over-estimate relevance; it can never cause a relevant resource to be classified lower than it
would otherwise be, and therefore preserves the guarantee stated in [[#discovery-result]]. Retaining such a construct as an
ordinary conjunctive pattern must be avoided, because `MINUS` and `SERVICE` patterns describe, respectively, solutions to
exclude and data held elsewhere; treating them as required patterns changes the result in both directions.

A client should report which constructs it discarded, including those inside a `UNION` branch, existence tests treated as
undecidable, and the [=shape targets=] and `sh:not` values of an [=input shape=] that could not be translated, so that a consumer can tell that the
result approximates the input.
If discarding leaves no [=star pattern=] at all, the rule in [[#algorithm-steps]] applies and no [=discovery result=] is
produced.


# Discovery result # {#discovery-result}

A <dfn id="discovery-result-definition">discovery result</dfn> expresses the degree to which a candidate resource's shapes satisfy the input constraints.
The following result values are ordered from highest to lowest relevance:

<table class="def">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Result</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><dfn>contained</dfn> (<code>CONTAINED</code>)</td>
            <td>Every input star pattern, including its required nested patterns, is fully covered by the resource shapes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><dfn>aligned</dfn> (<code>ALIGNED</code>)</td>
            <td>The input is not contained, but at least one root triple pattern matches an open resource shape. Because the shape is open, unmatched properties do not establish incompatibility.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><dfn>unaligned</dfn> (<code>UNALIGNED</code>)</td>
            <td>The input is neither contained nor aligned, but a root triple pattern matches a closed resource shape, or a match exists only in a nested star pattern. A predicate that a resource shape declares but whose [=star-pattern dependency=] was undecided rather than [=refuted=] counts as such a match.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><dfn>weakly rejected</dfn> (<code>WEAKLY_REJECTED</code>)</td>
            <td>No triple pattern matches, but at least one resource shape that [=describes=] the star pattern is open, or none describes it. The absence of a declared property is therefore not definitive evidence of incompatibility.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><dfn>rejected</dfn> (<code>REJECTED</code>)</td>
            <td>No triple pattern matches and every resource shape that [=describes=] the star pattern, of which there is at least one, is closed or contradicts it by forbidding a required predicate or requiring the predicate of a [=negated triple pattern=] (a [=negative contradiction=]), or there is no resource shape at all.</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

The relevance ordering is:

<p class="equation"><code>CONTAINED &gt; ALIGNED &gt; UNALIGNED &gt; WEAKLY_REJECTED &gt; REJECTED</code>.</p>

For an input containing multiple star patterns, the aggregate result must be the lowest result in this ordering among all
per-star-pattern results. A discovery client should order returned resources by this aggregate result. Resources with equal
aggregate results may be ordered using application-specific criteria. A client may omit [=rejected=] resources from the returned
subset, but must preserve their classification when they are included. Omitting them does not discard a resource that could have
satisfied the input constraints: [=rejected=] is assigned only when every [=resource shape=] that [=describes=] the star
pattern is closed or yields a [=negative contradiction=], and in both cases the shape positively establishes that no
conforming node satisfies the star pattern: a required property is absent, or the property of a [=negated triple pattern=]
is present. A shape with [=shape targets=] only describes a star pattern whose matching nodes are all its focus nodes, so its
constraints do apply to them.
An open shape reached only through
`sh:node` therefore prevents [=rejected=] just as an open [=resource shape=] does, unless it yields a
[=negative contradiction=].
A client must not omit resources classified [=weakly rejected=] on the same grounds, because an open shape that simply does not
declare a property is not evidence that the property is absent.

The per-star-pattern details and visit indications are supporting evidence rather than additional relevance degrees. In particular,
a resource shape may be marked as visitable after a partial binding even when the resource's aggregate result is [=unaligned=].

A [=discovery result=] computed from an input from which constructs were discarded ([[#out-of-profile]]) approximates a sub-input
of the one given, and should be reported together with the list of discarded constructs.

Note: The reference implementation currently exposes the misspelled identifier <code>UNALINGED</code> for
<code>UNALIGNED</code>. These identifiers denote the same result; serializations defined by this specification should use
<code>UNALIGNED</code>.

